(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "ADR-2608124200")
  (:adr/title "The stray clone held the only copies, and no remote ever had them")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-08-12")
  (:adr/supersedes "")
  (:adr/superseded-by "")
  (:adr/related (vector "ADR-2608122400" "ADR-2608123400"))
  (:adr/body
  "## What looked like a misplaced directory

`cloud-itonami/gftd-motion-actor` is registered at `orgs/cloud-itonami/` and a
clone of it also sat at `orgs/gftdcojp/`. The obvious reading is a leftover from
the org move: let west create the registered path, remove the stray.

**The premise was incomplete — both paths already existed**, the registered one
clean at its pin. Both slugs resolve to repository id `1298218954`, `fork:
false`, no parent: a rename redirect, not a fork. So it really was one repo at
two paths.

## The stray held the only copies of the content

Checked the way ADR-2608122400 prescribes — branch tips against the remote,
never `git log --branches --not --remotes`, which is meaningless in checkouts
that have no remote-tracking refs. Four branches exist server-side. **The
`git-annex` branch does not.**

Unique content: that branch, plus **5 annexed `.mp4` objects, 6.0 MB**. They
were the only copies anywhere, for two compounding reasons:

- **Annexed bytes never live in git objects**, and the branch that tracks them
  is absent from GitHub.
- **No B2 special remote was ever configured** — the branch's `uuid.log` names
  exactly one repository, the stray itself, and there is **no `remote.log` at
  all** — despite the west entry declaring `annex-remote: b2`. **The content was
  never published.**

And the registered sibling had **no `.git/annex` directory**, so all five of its
committed symlinks were **dangling**. The repo looked complete and resolved to
nothing.

**These are intended content, not regenerable output.** `.gitattributes` routes
every binary to annex by design and the pointers are committed on `main`. That
is the opposite of ADR-2608122400's 164 MB case, where `.gitignore` excluded the
files and the generation parameters survived in the sibling.

**The cause is visible in the manifest**: `group-filter` carries `-datalad`, so
this project is skipped by default `west update`, and its content only ever
arrives through the explicit annex-get path — which had nothing to fetch.

## What was done, and what deliberately was not

Each object was verified by MD5 and byte length against its own MD5E key (5/5),
copied into the registered checkout's annex store at identical key paths, and
re-verified after the copy (5/5). **All five committed symlinks now resolve.**
The `git-annex` branch was bundled (`git bundle verify`: complete history) with
a transcript written to `.git/stash-archive-<date>/` in **both** checkouts —
because deleting an orphan destroys any archive kept only inside it.

**Nothing was deleted, and nothing was published.** The content now sits in two
local checkouts and still in no remote. Publishing to B2 is the durable fix and
needs credentials; **only after that is removing the stray safe.** Note that
`cleanup-workflow.edn` already names `git-annex` under branches never to delete,
and excludes annex datasets from the automated lander.

## What this changes about the orphan audit

ADR-2608122400 classified 41 orphans and found 4 holding work, all stashes. That
audit asked about commits, working trees, stashes and branches. **It did not ask
whether annexed content existed only locally**, and for a `-datalad` project
that is precisely where the unique bytes live — outside git objects, in a branch
that may never have been pushed.

**A checkout can be byte-identical in git and still be the only place the data
exists.** Any future orphan retirement must check `.git/annex` and the
`git-annex` branch separately, and must not treat a clean `git status` as
evidence of nothing to lose.

## The VPN LoadBalancer pointer, landed the same pass

ADR-2608123400 concluded that the LoadBalancer is a leftover the design stopped
using. The owning repo's ADRs are **not silent, and one contradicts**: its
active design ADR authored the port mapping deliberately and, in the same table,
recorded the tunnel route that replaced it; its ledger still lists the load
balancer as live and says to protect it, true on 2026-06-16 and never revised;
a third records the cluster and its load balancers deleted on 2026-06-24. **No
ADR anywhere discusses ClusterIP for this service.**

The pointer was added by **editing the existing ADR rather than adding a file**:
that repo's own `CLAUDE.md` carries a Phase 0 freeze forbidding new files under
the numbered layer directories while explicitly permitting edits. `:last_verified`
was deliberately not bumped — what was verified is the pointer, not the whole
May ADR.

The manifest itself was **not** changed: `service.yaml` is still
`type: LoadBalancer`, and the precondition stands — the Worker secret may hold
either URL form, its SHAPE must be checked before applying, and the value was
not read.

## Two operational notes worth keeping

- That repo is **5.4 GiB**, roughly an hour to clone, so the change landed
  through the server-side git API write path that `cleanup-workflow.edn`
  prescribes for exactly this case. The shared checkout was never touched.
- The branch was cut from the **real remote tip**, not the west pin, which was 5
  commits behind. That checkout is detached at the pin, its remote is not named
  `origin`, and nothing was pushed from it.

## RESOLVED. Published, then retired — in that order

**Published.** The B2 special remote is configured and real, and all five
objects were confirmed present. Then the `git-annex` branch was pushed
(`babf541..481b085`) — **without which the content is in B2 and no fresh clone
can find it**, because the branch is what records where the objects live. Content
and the map to it are two separate publications and both are required.

**Then retired.** `orgs/gftdcojp/gftd-motion-actor` is removed, along with a
second worktree at `/private/tmp/gma` whose `.git` was a symlink into the
stray — **not recorded in the original audit, and deleting the stray blind would
have left it dangling.**

Three things in how it was checked are worth keeping:

- **`whereis` reports CLAIMS, not presence.** The agent probed B2 directly for
  each key rather than trusting the location log — which is exactly the right
  instinct, and the same class as everything else recorded today: the tool
  answers its own question correctly.
- **`main-2` in the log WAS the stray itself.** So the three copies were
  registered checkout, B2, and the doomed clone: deleting it took every object
  **3 to 2, never to 1.** Reading `3 copies` without resolving which repository
  each was would have made the deletion look safer than it was.
- **The archives had to be checked before the delete, not after.** A bundle of
  the annex branch lived inside both checkouts, and removing the stray destroys
  the copy inside it. The surviving one was `git bundle verify`-ed first.

`rm -rf` initially failed: **git-annex write-protects its objects.** The fix was
a `chmod` scoped behind a path guard — a filesystem delete of a doomed clone, not
`git annex drop`, and no gc or prune anywhere.

### The location log overstated redundancy afterwards, and that is now closed

Removing a clone from disk **does not retract its claim** — the location log is a
distributed claim log, so `whereis` kept reporting 3 copies when the truth was 2.
Left uncorrected that is a log which says the data is more redundant than it is,
which is the worst direction for this particular record to be wrong in.

`git annex dead 30a27285-…` retracts it, and the updated log was pushed
(`481b085..c74f6bf`). **`whereis` now reports 2 copies**, which is the truth.
HEAD unmoved, working tree clean throughout.")))
